# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Speed, velocity, acceleration
L.append({"id": "speed-velocity-and-acceleration", "title": "Speed, velocity and acceleration", "band": "Grade 8", "minutes": 32,
 "lede": "How fast, which way, and how quickly that's changing. The three ideas that describe every moving thing, from a jab to an 80,000-lb truck.",
 "sections": [
  T("## Distance vs displacement\n**Distance** is how much ground you covered. **Displacement** is how far you ended up from where you started, **and in which direction**.\n\nRun one full lap of a 400 m track: distance = **400 m**, displacement = **0 m**. You ended where you began.\n\nThat difference sounds picky. It's the whole key to the next idea."),
  T("## Speed vs velocity\n- **Speed** = distance ÷ time. It tells you *how fast*.\n- **Velocity** = displacement ÷ time. It tells you *how fast* **and which way**.\n\nQuantities with a direction are called **vectors** (velocity, displacement, force). Quantities without one are **scalars** (speed, distance, mass, time).\n\nA truck going 60 mph north and one going 60 mph south have the **same speed** and **different velocities**. If they're heading toward each other, that difference matters a lot."),
  T("## Units and conversions\nScience uses **meters per second** (m/s). Roads use miles per hour (mph). The bridge:\n\n**1 mph ≈ 0.447 m/s** and **1 m/s ≈ 2.24 mph**\n\n| mph | m/s | Feel |\n|---|---|---|\n| 3 | 1.3 | walking |\n| 15 | 6.7 | sprinting human |\n| 30 | 13.4 | city street |\n| 60 | 26.8 | highway |\n| 70 | 31.3 | interstate |\n\nA useful image: at 60 mph you cover about **27 meters every second**, a little more than the length of a full tractor-trailer."),
  E("Average speed", "You drive 150 miles in 2.5 hours. What's your average speed?", ["Average speed = total distance ÷ total time.", "150 ÷ 2.5 = 60."], "**60 mph**. Average speed tells you nothing about the moments you were stopped or going 75."),
  T("## Acceleration\n**Acceleration** is how fast velocity **changes**:\n\n**acceleration = change in velocity ÷ time**, written **a = Δv ÷ t** (Δ, \"delta,\" means *change in*)\n\nUnits: (m/s) per second = **m/s²**.\n\nA car going from 0 to 27 m/s (60 mph) in 9 seconds accelerates at 27 ÷ 9 = **3 m/s²**: it gains 3 m/s of speed every second.\n\nThree ways to accelerate, because velocity has speed **and** direction:\n1. **Speed up**\n2. **Slow down** (negative acceleration, or deceleration)\n3. **Turn**, even at constant speed. Your direction changes, so your velocity changes."),
  CHECK("A truck drives around a curve at a steady 45 mph. Is it accelerating?", ["No, its speed is constant", "Yes, its direction is changing", "Only if it brakes", "Only if the road is banked"], 1,
        "Velocity includes direction. Turning changes the direction, so the truck **is** accelerating, toward the inside of the curve. That's why loose cargo slides outward."),
  T("## Gravity: the acceleration everyone shares\nNear Earth's surface, anything falling freely (ignoring air) accelerates at **g ≈ 9.8 m/s²**, regardless of mass. A bowling ball and a baseball dropped together land together. Galileo figured this out around 1600; Apollo 15 astronaut David Scott proved it on the Moon in 1971 by dropping a hammer and a feather: with no air, they landed at the same time."),
  T("## Reading motion graphs\n| Graph | Slope means | Area under it means |\n|---|---|---|\n| Position vs time | **velocity** | (not used) |\n| Velocity vs time | **acceleration** | **displacement** |\n\nA flat line on a position-time graph = standing still. A flat line on a velocity-time graph = constant velocity (zero acceleration). Steeper = faster change."),
  T("## The equations for constant acceleration\nWhen acceleration stays constant, three equations connect everything (v₀ = starting velocity, v = final velocity, a = acceleration, t = time, d = displacement):\n\n1. **v = v₀ + a·t**\n2. **d = v₀·t + ½·a·t²**\n3. **v² = v₀² + 2·a·d**\n\nPick the one that has the three things you know and the one you want."),
  E("Why speed kills: braking distance", "A truck at 25 m/s brakes at a steady 5 m/s². How far does it travel while stopping? What if it was going 50 m/s?",
    ["Use v² = v₀² + 2·a·d with final v = 0 and a = −5 (slowing down).", "0 = 25² − 2·5·d → d = 625 ÷ 10 = **62.5 m**.", "At 50 m/s: d = 50² ÷ 10 = 2,500 ÷ 10 = **250 m**."],
    "**Double the speed, four times the braking distance.** Distance grows with speed **squared**. That's physics, not opinion, and it's why speed limits for trucks exist."),
  N("Common traps", "- **Average speed isn't the average of the speeds.** Drive 60 mi at 60 mph and 60 mi back at 40 mph: total time = 1 h + 1.5 h = 2.5 h, total distance 120 mi, average = **48 mph**, not 50. You spent more *time* at the slower speed.\n- **Negative acceleration doesn't always mean slowing down.** It means acceleration points in the negative direction. If you're already moving in the negative direction, it speeds you up.\n- **Heavier objects don't fall faster** (without air). Air resistance is what makes a feather drift.")
 ],
 "practice": [
  NUM("A runner covers 400 m in 80 s. Average speed in m/s?", 5, "400 ÷ 80 = 5 m/s.", unit="m/s"),
  NUM("Convert 70 mph to m/s (round to 1 decimal).", 31.3, "70 × 0.447 ≈ 31.3 m/s.", tol=0.15, unit="m/s"),
  MC("Which is a **vector**?", ["speed", "distance", "velocity", "time"], 2, "Velocity has a direction. The others are just size."),
  NUM("A car goes from 0 to 24 m/s in 8 s. Acceleration?", 3, "a = Δv ÷ t = 24 ÷ 8 = 3 m/s².", unit="m/s²"),
  MC("You run one full lap around a 400 m track in 100 s. Your average **velocity** is…", ["4 m/s", "0 m/s", "400 m/s", "1 m/s"], 1, "Displacement is 0 (you ended where you started), so average velocity is 0. Average **speed** is 4 m/s."),
  NUM("A rock falls from rest for 3 s. How fast is it going? (g = 9.8 m/s², ignore air)", 29.4, "v = v₀ + a·t = 0 + 9.8 × 3 = 29.4 m/s.", tol=0.05, unit="m/s"),
  MC("On a **velocity-time** graph, the slope tells you…", ["position", "acceleration", "distance", "mass"], 1, "Slope of velocity vs time = change in velocity ÷ time = acceleration."),
  MC("Which is **not** acceleration?", ["Speeding up on an on-ramp", "Braking at a light", "Driving a curve at a steady 40 mph", "Cruising straight at a steady 65 mph"], 3, "Constant speed **and** constant direction = constant velocity = zero acceleration."),
  NUM("You drive 120 miles to a delivery at 60 mph and back at 40 mph. What's your average speed for the round trip?", 48,
      "Time out = 120 ÷ 60 = 2 h. Time back = 120 ÷ 40 = 3 h. Total: 240 mi ÷ 5 h = **48 mph**. You spent more time at the slow speed, so the average leans toward it.",
      level="challenge", hint="Average speed = total distance ÷ total time. Find each leg's time first.", unit="mph",
      steps=["Out: 120 ÷ 60 = 2 h.", "Back: 120 ÷ 40 = 3 h.", "Total: 240 mi in 5 h.", "240 ÷ 5 = 48 mph."]),
  NUM("A truck's braking distance at 20 m/s is 40 m. Same brakes, same road, at 30 m/s. Braking distance?", 90,
      "Braking distance grows with speed **squared**. Speed × 1.5 → distance × 1.5² = 2.25. 40 × 2.25 = **90 m**.",
      level="challenge", hint="If speed goes up by 1.5×, distance goes up by 1.5 squared.", unit="m",
      steps=["Ratio of speeds: 30 ÷ 20 = 1.5.", "Distance scales with speed squared: 1.5² = 2.25.", "40 × 2.25 = 90 m."]),
  NUM("A car brakes from 30 m/s to a stop in 75 m. What is the size of its deceleration?", 6,
      "v² = v₀² + 2·a·d → 0 = 900 + 2·a·75 → a = −900 ÷ 150 = −6 m/s². The size is **6 m/s²**.",
      level="challenge", hint="Use v² = v₀² + 2·a·d with final velocity 0.", unit="m/s²"),
  MC("A ball is thrown straight up. At the very top, for an instant, its velocity is 0. What is its acceleration there?", ["0", "9.8 m/s² downward", "9.8 m/s² upward", "It depends on how hard it was thrown"], 1,
     "Gravity never switches off. Velocity is momentarily 0, but it's still **changing** at 9.8 m/s² downward, which is why the ball starts falling. Zero velocity ≠ zero acceleration.",
     level="challenge", hint="Is gravity still pulling at the top?"),
 ],
 "sources": [OSP, OSCP]})

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Forces and Newton's laws
L.append({"id": "forces-and-newton-s-laws", "title": "Forces and Newton's laws", "band": "Grade 8", "minutes": 32,
 "lede": "Three laws, written in 1687, still explain every push, pull, punch and crash. Learn them the way an engineer does.",
 "sections": [
  T("## What a force is\nA **force** is a push or a pull. It has a size and a direction (it's a vector), and it's measured in **newtons (N)**. One newton is about the weight of a small apple, roughly 0.22 lb.\n\nObjects usually have several forces on them at once. What matters is the **net force**: all the forces added up, with direction.\n\n- 100 N right + 100 N left = **0 N net**: balanced. No change in motion.\n- 100 N right + 60 N left = **40 N right**: unbalanced. The object accelerates to the right."),
  T("## Newton's first law: inertia\n**An object keeps doing what it's doing, staying still or moving in a straight line at a steady speed, unless a net force acts on it.**\n\nThat tendency to keep doing the same thing is **inertia**, and the more **mass** something has, the more inertia.\n\nThis is the most misunderstood law in physics. People assume things *naturally* slow down. They don't. They slow down because **friction and air resistance** are forces acting on them. In space, a thrown wrench flies forever.\n\nIn a truck: when you brake hard, the truck slows, but an unsecured load **keeps moving forward** at the old speed until something stops it, often the cab. That's not the load *flying forward*; it's the truck *stopping* underneath it. It's why cargo securement rules exist."),
  CHECK("A hockey puck slides across smooth ice and slowly stops. Why does it stop?", ["Moving objects naturally run out of motion", "Friction from the ice and air act against it", "Its inertia runs out", "Gravity pulls it backward"], 1,
        "Without a force, it would slide forever. **Friction** is the force that stops it. Inertia never runs out."),
  T("## Newton's second law: F = m·a\n**net force = mass × acceleration**, or **F = m·a**.\n\nRearranged, **a = F ÷ m**: the same force accelerates a light object a lot and a heavy one a little.\n\n| Same 2,000 N push on… | Mass | Acceleration |\n|---|---|---|\n| a 100 kg person on a cart | 100 kg | 20 m/s² |\n| a 1,500 kg car | 1,500 kg | 1.3 m/s² |\n| a 36,000 kg loaded truck | 36,000 kg | 0.06 m/s² |\n\nRun it in reverse for stopping: a fully loaded truck needs about **24 times** the braking force of a car to slow down equally fast. Brakes that can't deliver that just stop the truck over a longer distance."),
  T("## Mass vs weight\n- **Mass** = how much matter (kg). It's the same everywhere.\n- **Weight** = the force of gravity on that mass (N). **W = m·g**.\n\nAn 80 kg fighter weighs 80 × 9.8 = **784 N** on Earth. On the Moon (g = 1.62 m/s²) he'd weigh about **130 N**, but his mass is still 80 kg. He'd be just as hard to push sideways, just much easier to lift.\n\n(Bathroom scales show *pounds* or *kg* by quietly converting from the force they actually measure.)"),
  T("## Newton's third law: action and reaction\n**When object A pushes on object B, B pushes back on A with an equal force in the opposite direction.**\n\n- Throw a punch into a heavy bag: the bag pushes back on your fist just as hard. That's what you feel in your wrist.\n- A truck's tires push **backward** on the road; the road pushes the truck **forward**. On ice, the tires can't push back hard enough, so you can't go anywhere.\n\nIf the forces are equal and opposite, why does anything move? Because they act on **different objects**. The bag's push acts on your fist; your push acts on the bag. Forces only cancel when they act on the **same** object."),
  CHECK("A small car hits a big truck head-on. Which statement is true?", ["The truck hits the car harder", "The car and truck push on each other with equal force", "The car hits the truck harder because it's faster", "Only the car feels a force"], 1,
        "Third law: **equal force** on each. The car is wrecked worse because the same force on **less mass** means far more acceleration (a = F ÷ m), and acceleration is what injures."),
  T("## Friction\n**Friction** is the force that resists surfaces sliding across each other. Two kinds:\n\n- **Static friction**: holds things still or keeps a rolling tire from slipping. It's **stronger**.\n- **Kinetic friction**: acts once something is sliding. It's **weaker**.\n\nThat's the reason **anti-lock brakes (ABS)** exist. A rolling tire grips the road with static friction. A locked, skidding tire slides with weaker kinetic friction, and it can't steer. ABS pulses the brakes so the tires keep rolling, keeping the stronger grip.\n\nFriction depends on how hard the surfaces are pressed together (the **normal force**) and on the surfaces themselves (rubber on dry asphalt grips far better than rubber on ice)."),
  E("Net force and acceleration", "A 1,200 kg car's engine pushes it forward with 3,000 N. Air and road friction push back with 600 N. What's its acceleration?",
    ["Net force = 3,000 − 600 = 2,400 N forward.", "a = F ÷ m = 2,400 ÷ 1,200 = 2 m/s²."], "**2 m/s² forward.** Always find the **net** force first."),
  N("Common traps", "- **\"Motion needs a force.\"** No: **changes** in motion need a force. Steady motion needs none.\n- **Action-reaction pairs never cancel each other,** because they act on different objects.\n- **Heavier ≠ falls faster.** More mass means more weight (force), but also more inertia. In F = m·a, the mass cancels: everything falls at g.")
 ],
 "practice": [
  NUM("What net force gives a 50 kg cart an acceleration of 4 m/s²?", 200, "F = m·a = 50 × 4 = 200 N.", unit="N"),
  NUM("A 2,000 N net force acts on a 500 kg trailer. Acceleration?", 4, "a = F ÷ m = 2,000 ÷ 500 = 4 m/s².", unit="m/s²"),
  NUM("Weight of a 70 kg person on Earth? (g = 9.8 m/s²)", 686, "W = m·g = 70 × 9.8 = 686 N.", unit="N"),
  MC("Forces of 300 N east and 300 N west act on a box sitting still. The box…", ["accelerates east", "stays still", "accelerates west", "has no forces on it"], 1, "Net force = 0, so no change in motion."),
  MC("When a bus stops suddenly, standing riders lurch forward because of…", ["a forward force from the bus", "inertia", "gravity", "the third law"], 1, "Their bodies keep moving forward while the bus stops: **inertia**. Nothing pushes them forward."),
  MC("A rocket in space pushes hot gas backward. What pushes the rocket forward?", ["The air behind it", "The gas pushing back on the rocket", "Gravity", "Nothing; it just moves"], 1, "Third law: rocket pushes gas back, **gas pushes rocket forward**. No air needed."),
  MC("Your mass on the Moon compared to Earth is…", ["about 1/6", "the same", "zero", "6 times bigger"], 1, "**Mass** doesn't change. Your **weight** drops to about 1/6."),
  MC("Why do anti-lock brakes help a truck stop and steer?", ["They make the brakes stronger", "They keep tires rolling, using stronger static friction", "They add weight to the wheels", "They reduce air resistance"], 1, "A skidding tire uses weaker kinetic friction and can't steer."),
  NUM("A 900 kg car's engine pushes with 2,700 N while friction pushes back with 900 N. Acceleration?", 2,
      "Net force = 2,700 − 900 = 1,800 N. a = 1,800 ÷ 900 = **2 m/s²**.",
      level="challenge", hint="Find the net force first.", unit="m/s²"),
  NUM("A loaded truck (36,000 kg) and a car (1,500 kg) both need to slow at 4 m/s². How many times more braking force does the truck need?", 24,
      "F = m·a with the same a, so force scales with mass: 36,000 ÷ 1,500 = **24 times**.",
      level="challenge", hint="Same acceleration. What's left in F = m·a?"),
  MC("You stand on a scale in an elevator that **accelerates upward**. The scale reads…", ["your normal weight", "more than your normal weight", "less than your normal weight", "zero"], 1,
     "To accelerate you upward, the floor must push up **harder** than gravity pulls down: N − m·g = m·a, so N = m(g + a). The scale reads that push.",
     level="challenge", hint="For you to accelerate up, which force has to be bigger: the floor's push or gravity's pull?"),
  MC("A horse pulls a cart. By the third law, the cart pulls back on the horse equally hard. So how does the cart move?", ["It can't; the forces cancel", "The forces act on different objects; the horse pushes the ground harder than the cart pulls back on it", "The horse is heavier", "The third law doesn't apply to animals"], 1,
     "Look at the **horse** alone: the ground pushes it forward (third-law reaction to its hooves pushing back) harder than the cart pulls it back, so the horse accelerates. Look at the **cart**: the horse's pull is bigger than friction on the cart, so it accelerates too.",
     level="challenge", hint="Forces only cancel when they act on the same object. List the forces on the horse alone."),
 ],
 "sources": [OSP, OSCP]})

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Energy
L.append({"id": "energy-and-its-forms", "title": "Energy and its forms", "band": "Grade 8", "minutes": 32,
 "lede": "Energy is never made or destroyed, only moved and changed. Follow it and you can explain a punch, a crash, a power bill and the food on your plate.",
 "sections": [
  T("## What energy is\n**Energy** is the ability to do **work**: to move something by pushing it through a distance. Both are measured in **joules (J)**.\n\n**Work = force × distance** (with the force in the direction of motion)\n\nPush a stalled car with 500 N for 10 m and you've done 500 × 10 = **5,000 J** of work, transferring 5,000 J of energy into the car. Push a wall all day and you do **zero** work on it (it doesn't move), even though you're exhausted. Your muscles burn energy internally, but none goes into the wall."),
  T("## Kinetic energy: energy of motion\n**KE = ½ · m · v²**\n\nTwo things to notice:\n- Double the **mass** → double the energy.\n- Double the **speed** → **four times** the energy, because speed is squared.\n\nThat square is why speed matters so much in crashes and in fights. A punch's energy depends far more on hand speed than on arm mass. And all that kinetic energy has to go somewhere when a truck stops: into the brakes as **heat**. That's why brakes fade on long downhill grades: they overheat."),
  E("Car vs truck at the same speed", "Both go 25 m/s (about 56 mph). Car: 1,500 kg. Loaded truck: 36,000 kg.",
    ["Car: ½ × 1,500 × 25² = ½ × 1,500 × 625 = **468,750 J**.", "Truck: ½ × 36,000 × 625 = **11,250,000 J** (11.25 megajoules)."],
    "The truck carries **24 times** the energy. Its brakes have to turn all of it into heat to stop."),
  T("## Potential energy: stored energy\nEnergy stored by position or condition:\n\n- **Gravitational**: **PE = m · g · h**. Lift something higher and you store energy in it.\n- **Elastic**: a stretched band, a compressed spring, a drawn bow.\n- **Chemical**: stored in the bonds of fuel, food and batteries.\n- **Nuclear**: stored in atomic nuclei; released in the Sun and in power plants."),
  T("## Conservation of energy\n**Energy cannot be created or destroyed. It only changes form or moves.** This is one of the most tested laws in all of science; no experiment has ever broken it.\n\nA dropped rock: **PE at the top** → turns into **KE** on the way down → at impact, turns into **sound, heat and deformation**. The total never changes.\n\nSo where does energy go when things \"lose\" it? Almost always into **thermal energy**: random motion of molecules, i.e., heat. It's still there. It's just spread out and hard to use again."),
  CHECK("A roller coaster car (no friction) starts at rest at the top of a 45 m hill. At the bottom, its kinetic energy equals…", ["zero", "its potential energy at the top", "half its potential energy at the top", "twice its potential energy at the top"], 1,
        "With no friction, **all** the PE becomes KE. Energy is conserved."),
  E("Speed from height", "A wrench falls off a 20 m bridge. How fast is it going when it hits the water? (Ignore air.)",
    ["PE at top = KE at bottom: m·g·h = ½·m·v².", "Mass cancels: v² = 2·g·h = 2 × 9.8 × 20 = 392.", "v = √392 ≈ 19.8 m/s."], "**About 19.8 m/s** (≈ 44 mph). Notice the mass canceled: a wrench and a bolt hit at the same speed."),
  T("## Power: how fast energy moves\n**Power = energy ÷ time**, measured in **watts (W)**. 1 W = 1 J per second.\n\n- **1 horsepower ≈ 746 W.** A 500 hp truck engine can deliver about 373,000 J every second.\n- Your power bill charges by the **kilowatt-hour (kWh)**: 1,000 W running for 1 hour = **3.6 million J**.\n\nTwo people can do the same **work** climbing a flight of stairs; the one who sprints up uses more **power**."),
  T("## Food is energy too\nA food **Calorie** (capital C) is a kilocalorie: **1 Calorie = 4,184 J**.\n\nA 3,100-Calorie day is about **13 million joules**, roughly the kinetic energy of that loaded truck at highway speed. When you train, your muscles turn only about 20–25% of the energy they burn into motion; the rest becomes body heat. That's why you get hot when you train."),
  T("## Efficiency\n**Efficiency = useful energy out ÷ total energy in.**\n\nNo machine is 100% efficient; some energy always ends up as heat. Rough numbers:\n\n| Machine | Efficiency |\n|---|---|\n| Gasoline car engine | about 20–35% |\n| Big diesel truck engine | about 40–45% at its best |\n| Electric motor | about 85–95% |\n| LED bulb (energy → light) | far better than old incandescent bulbs (~5%) |\n\nSo when you fill up a gas car, most of the fuel's energy heats the engine and the air."),
  N("Common traps", "- **Work needs motion in the direction of the force.** Carrying a box across a level room does almost no work on the box, physics-wise, because you push up and it moves sideways.\n- **\"Using up\" energy** really means turning it into less useful forms, mostly heat.\n- **KE grows with v², not v.** Most people underestimate how much more dangerous 80 mph is than 60 mph: (80/60)² ≈ 1.78, so about 78% more energy.")
 ],
 "practice": [
  NUM("You push a crate with 300 N for 4 m. Work done?", 1200, "W = F × d = 300 × 4 = 1,200 J.", unit="J"),
  NUM("Kinetic energy of a 2 kg ball moving at 3 m/s?", 9, "KE = ½ × 2 × 3² = 9 J.", unit="J"),
  NUM("Potential energy of a 10 kg box on a 5 m shelf? (g = 9.8)", 490, "PE = m·g·h = 10 × 9.8 × 5 = 490 J.", unit="J"),
  MC("If you double a car's speed, its kinetic energy becomes…", ["2 times as large", "4 times as large", "half as large", "unchanged"], 1, "KE has v² in it: 2² = 4."),
  MC("Where does most of a truck's kinetic energy go when it brakes to a stop?", ["It's destroyed", "Heat in the brakes", "Back into the fuel", "Sound"], 1, "Friction in the brakes turns KE into **thermal energy**."),
  NUM("A 1,200 W microwave runs for 30 seconds. Energy used?", 36000, "Energy = power × time = 1,200 × 30 = 36,000 J.", unit="J"),
  MC("A machine takes in 1,000 J and puts out 250 J of useful work. Its efficiency is…", ["25%", "75%", "250%", "4%"], 0, "250 ÷ 1,000 = 0.25 = 25%."),
  MC("You hold a 20 kg barbell overhead, perfectly still, for 30 seconds. Work done **on the barbell**:", ["600 J", "0 J", "196 J", "5,880 J"], 1, "It doesn't move, so **no work** is done on it. Your muscles still burn energy internally."),
  NUM("A stone falls from rest off a 45 m cliff. Speed at the bottom? (g = 9.8, ignore air; round to 1 decimal)", 29.7,
      "m·g·h = ½·m·v² → v = √(2·g·h) = √(2 × 9.8 × 45) = √882 ≈ **29.7 m/s**.",
      level="challenge", hint="Set PE at the top equal to KE at the bottom. The mass cancels.", tol=0.1, unit="m/s"),
  NUM("A 1,500 W space heater runs 4 hours a day for 30 days at $0.15 per kWh. Cost in dollars?", 27,
      "1.5 kW × 4 h = 6 kWh per day. × 30 days = 180 kWh. × $0.15 = **$27**.",
      level="challenge", hint="Convert watts to kilowatts, multiply by hours, then by the price.", tol=0.01, unit="dollars",
      steps=["1,500 W = 1.5 kW.", "1.5 kW × 4 h = 6 kWh per day.", "6 × 30 = 180 kWh.", "180 × 0.15 = 27."]),
  MC("A truck goes from 60 mph to 80 mph. Its kinetic energy goes up by about…", ["33%", "78%", "20%", "100%"], 1,
     "KE ∝ v². (80 ÷ 60)² = (4/3)² = 16/9 ≈ 1.78, so about **78% more** energy, and about 78% more braking distance.",
     level="challenge", hint="Square the ratio of the speeds."),
  NUM("A 3,100-Calorie day of food is how many **million** joules? (1 Calorie = 4,184 J; round to 1 decimal)", 13.0,
      "3,100 × 4,184 = 12,970,400 J ≈ **13.0 million J**.",
      level="challenge", hint="Multiply, then divide by 1,000,000.", tol=0.06, unit="million J"),
 ],
 "sources": [OSP, OSCP, {"title": "U.S. Energy Information Administration: Energy explained", "url": "https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/"}]})

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Waves, sound and light
L.append({"id": "waves-sound-and-light", "title": "Waves, sound and light", "band": "Grade 8", "minutes": 32,
 "lede": "A wave moves energy without moving stuff along with it. One equation, v = f·λ, explains your voice, your Wi-Fi and the color of the sky.",
 "sections": [
  T("## What a wave is\nA **wave** is a disturbance that carries **energy** from place to place **without carrying matter along**. When a stadium does \"the wave,\" the wave travels around the stadium but every fan stays in their seat.\n\nTwo kinds of motion:\n- **Transverse**: the medium moves **perpendicular** to the wave's direction (a rope flicked up and down, light).\n- **Longitudinal**: the medium moves **back and forth along** the wave's direction (sound: air squeezed and stretched)."),
  T("## Measuring a wave\n| Term | Meaning | Unit |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Amplitude** | how big the disturbance is | depends |\n| **Wavelength (λ)** | distance from one crest to the next | m |\n| **Frequency (f)** | waves passing per second | hertz (Hz) |\n| **Period (T)** | time for one wave | s |\n\nFrequency and period are flips of each other: **T = 1 ÷ f**. A 50 Hz wave has a period of 1/50 = 0.02 s.\n\nAnd the master equation:\n\n**wave speed = frequency × wavelength**, or **v = f · λ**"),
  CHECK("If a wave's frequency doubles while its speed stays the same, its wavelength…", ["doubles", "is cut in half", "stays the same", "quadruples"], 1,
        "v = f·λ. If v is fixed and f doubles, λ must halve. More waves per second means each one is shorter."),
  T("## Sound\nSound is a **longitudinal** wave of pressure. It needs a **medium** (air, water, steel) to travel through. In space, no one can hear you scream: there's nothing to carry the wave.\n\n| Medium | Speed of sound (approx.) |\n|---|---|\n| Air at 20 °C | 343 m/s |\n| Water | 1,480 m/s |\n| Steel | 5,900 m/s |\n\nSound goes **faster in denser, stiffer materials** because the particles are tightly linked and pass the push along quickly. That's why you can hear a train in the rails before you hear it in the air.\n\n- **Pitch** = frequency. Human ears hear roughly **20 Hz to 20,000 Hz**.\n- **Loudness** = amplitude, measured in **decibels (dB)**. The scale is logarithmic: **+10 dB = 10 times the intensity**, which sounds about twice as loud."),
  E("How far away was the lightning?", "You see lightning, then hear thunder 6 seconds later.",
    ["Light arrives almost instantly. Sound travels about 343 m/s.", "Distance = 343 × 6 ≈ 2,060 m."], "**About 2 km.** Rule of thumb: every **3 seconds ≈ 1 km**, or every **5 seconds ≈ 1 mile**."),
  T("## The Doppler effect\nWhen a source of sound moves **toward** you, its waves bunch up: shorter wavelength, **higher pitch**. Moving **away**, they stretch out: **lower pitch**. That's the *neeeee-owww* of a passing siren.\n\nPolice radar uses the same effect with radio waves to measure your speed, and astronomers use it with light to measure how fast galaxies are moving away from us."),
  T("## Light and the electromagnetic spectrum\nLight is a **transverse electromagnetic wave**. Unlike sound, it needs **no medium**: it crosses empty space at the fastest speed in the universe:\n\n**c ≈ 300,000,000 m/s** (3 × 10⁸ m/s)\n\nVisible light is a tiny slice of the **electromagnetic spectrum**. From long wavelength / low energy to short wavelength / high energy:\n\n| Type | Example use |\n|---|---|\n| Radio | FM, AM, CB radio |\n| Microwave | cooking, Wi-Fi, cell phones |\n| Infrared | heat, TV remotes, night vision |\n| **Visible** (red → violet, ~700 to ~400 nm) | sight |\n| Ultraviolet | sunburn, black lights |\n| X-ray | medical imaging |\n| Gamma | nuclear reactions, cancer treatment |\n\nHigher frequency means more energy per photon. That's why UV can burn skin and radio can't."),
  E("FM radio wavelength", "An FM station broadcasts at 100 MHz (100,000,000 Hz). What's its wavelength?",
    ["λ = v ÷ f = 3 × 10⁸ ÷ 1 × 10⁸.", "λ = 3 m."], "**3 meters.** Radio antennas are often sized to a fraction of the wavelength they receive."),
  T("## Reflection and refraction\n- **Reflection**: a wave bounces off a surface. Mirrors, echoes, sonar.\n- **Refraction**: a wave **bends** when it passes into a material where it travels at a different speed. A straw in water looks broken because light slows down in water. Lenses in glasses and cameras use refraction to focus light.\n\nWhite light refracts by slightly different amounts for each color, which is how a prism, or a raindrop, splits it into a rainbow."),
  N("Common traps", "- **Waves carry energy, not matter.** Ocean waves move water up and down far more than forward.\n- **Sound vs light speed:** light is about **875,000 times** faster than sound in air.\n- **Louder ≠ higher.** Loudness is amplitude; pitch is frequency. They're independent.")
 ],
 "practice": [
  NUM("A wave has frequency 5 Hz and wavelength 2 m. Its speed?", 10, "v = f·λ = 5 × 2 = 10 m/s.", unit="m/s"),
  NUM("A sound wave in air (343 m/s) has frequency 343 Hz. Wavelength?", 1, "λ = v ÷ f = 343 ÷ 343 = 1 m.", unit="m"),
  MC("Sound can't travel through…", ["water", "steel", "a vacuum (empty space)", "air"], 2, "Sound needs a medium to push through."),
  MC("The **pitch** of a sound is set by its…", ["amplitude", "frequency", "speed", "medium"], 1, "Higher frequency = higher pitch."),
  NUM("Thunder arrives 9 seconds after the lightning. About how many km away was the strike?", 3, "Every 3 s ≈ 1 km: 9 ÷ 3 = 3 km.", tol=0.15, unit="km"),
  MC("Which has the **most** energy per photon?", ["radio", "visible red", "ultraviolet", "infrared"], 2, "Order by frequency: radio < infrared < visible < **UV**."),
  MC("A siren moving **away** from you sounds…", ["higher-pitched", "lower-pitched", "the same", "louder"], 1, "Waves stretch out behind a moving source: longer wavelength, lower frequency."),
  MC("A straw looks bent in a glass of water because of…", ["reflection", "refraction", "the Doppler effect", "diffraction"], 1, "Light changes speed going from water to air and **bends**."),
  NUM("Wi-Fi uses 2.4 GHz (2.4 × 10⁹ Hz). Wavelength in **centimeters**? (c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s)", 12.5,
      "λ = 3 × 10⁸ ÷ 2.4 × 10⁹ = 0.125 m = **12.5 cm**.",
      level="challenge", hint="λ = c ÷ f, then convert meters to centimeters.", tol=0.05, unit="cm"),
  NUM("A 20 Hz bass note has what period, in **milliseconds**?", 50,
      "T = 1 ÷ f = 1 ÷ 20 = 0.05 s = **50 ms**.",
      level="challenge", hint="Period is 1 divided by frequency.", unit="ms"),
  MC("A sound goes from 60 dB to 80 dB. Its intensity increases by a factor of…", ["2", "20", "100", "1.33"], 2,
     "Each +10 dB = ×10 intensity. +20 dB = 10 × 10 = **100 times** the intensity (it sounds about 4 times as loud).",
     level="challenge", hint="+10 dB means ×10. How many tens are in 20?"),
  MC("Why can you hear a distant train through the rails before you hear it through the air?", ["The rails amplify the sound", "Sound travels much faster in steel than in air", "Light from the train reaches you first", "Steel lowers the pitch"], 1,
     "About 5,900 m/s in steel vs 343 m/s in air: over 17 times faster. Stiff, dense materials pass the vibration along quickly.",
     level="challenge", hint="Compare the speed-of-sound table."),
 ],
 "sources": [OSP, OSCP, {"title": "NASA Science: Introduction to the Electromagnetic Spectrum", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/ems/01_intro/"}]})

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Electricity and circuits
L.append({"id": "electricity-and-circuits", "title": "Electricity and circuits", "band": "Grade 8", "minutes": 32,
 "lede": "Voltage pushes, current flows, resistance fights back. Master V = I·R and you can read a battery, size a fuse and check your power bill.",
 "sections": [
  T("## The three quantities\n| Quantity | What it is | Unit | Water-pipe picture |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Voltage (V)** | the electrical *push*: energy per unit of charge | volt (V) | water pressure |\n| **Current (I)** | how much charge flows per second | ampere, \"amp\" (A) | flow rate |\n| **Resistance (R)** | how much the path fights the flow | ohm (Ω) | a narrow pipe |\n\nElectric current is the flow of **charge**, carried in wires by electrons. A circuit is a **closed loop**: a source (battery, outlet), a path (wires) and a load (bulb, motor). Break the loop anywhere and the current stops everywhere in it. That's what a switch does."),
  T("## Ohm's law\n**V = I · R**: voltage = current × resistance.\n\nRearranged: **I = V ÷ R** and **R = V ÷ I**.\n\nRead *I = V ÷ R* as a sentence: **more push, more flow; more resistance, less flow.**"),
  E("Current through a bulb", "A 120 V outlet powers a lamp with 240 Ω resistance. What current flows?", ["I = V ÷ R = 120 ÷ 240.", "I = 0.5 A."], "**0.5 amps.**"),
  T("## Power and energy\n**Power = voltage × current**, or **P = V · I**, in watts.\n\nThat same lamp: P = 120 × 0.5 = **60 W**.\n\nCombine with Ohm's law and you get **P = I² · R**: power wasted as heat in a wire grows with current **squared**. That's why power companies send electricity over long distances at very **high voltage**: for the same power, high voltage means low current, and low current means far less energy lost as heat in the lines.\n\nYour bill counts **energy**: kilowatts × hours = **kWh**."),
  CHECK("A 1,200 W hair dryer runs on 120 V. What current does it draw?", ["0.1 A", "10 A", "1,080 A", "144,000 A"], 1, "I = P ÷ V = 1,200 ÷ 120 = **10 A**."),
  T("## Series circuits: one path\nIn a **series** circuit, components sit one after another on a single loop.\n\n- The **same current** flows through every part.\n- **Resistances add**: R total = R₁ + R₂ + R₃...\n- The battery's voltage is **split** among the parts.\n- **One break kills everything.** That's why old Christmas lights went dark when one bulb burned out."),
  T("## Parallel circuits: several paths\nIn a **parallel** circuit, each component has its own branch back to the source.\n\n- Each branch gets the **full voltage**.\n- Currents in the branches **add up** to the total.\n- **Adding a branch lowers the total resistance**: more paths, easier flow. For two equal resistors, the total is **half** of one. In general: **1/R total = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + ...**\n- One branch can fail and the others keep working.\n\n**Your house is wired in parallel.** Every outlet gets 120 V, and every device you plug in adds current to the circuit. Plug in too many and the total current passes the breaker's rating (often 15 or 20 A), and it trips to keep the wires from overheating."),
  E("Truck batteries: series vs parallel", "Many heavy trucks carry several 12 V batteries. How should they be connected?",
    ["**Parallel** (positive to positive, negative to negative): voltage stays **12 V**, but the available current and capacity add up. That's what a 12 V truck uses to crank a big diesel.", "**Series** (positive to negative): voltages **add**. Two 12 V batteries = **24 V**. Some trucks and military vehicles run 24 V systems."],
    "Same batteries, different wiring, different result. Hooking up jumper cables wrong can create a short circuit with enormous current, which is why the order matters."),
  T("## Safety: it's the current\nCurrent through the body is what injures. Around **0.1 A (100 mA)** passing through the chest can throw the heart out of rhythm. Your skin's resistance is what usually keeps current low, and wet skin has far less resistance. That's the physics behind keeping electricity away from water and behind **GFCI** outlets in kitchens and bathrooms, which cut power in a fraction of a second when current leaks to ground.\n\n- **Fuses and breakers** protect **wires** from overheating.\n- **GFCIs** protect **people**.\n\nIn the US, wall outlets supply about **120 V AC at 60 Hz**: alternating current that reverses direction 60 times a second. Batteries supply **DC**: one direction."),
  N("Common traps", "- **Current isn't \"used up.\"** The same current that leaves the battery returns to it. What gets used is **energy**.\n- **Adding resistors in parallel decreases total resistance.** It feels backwards until you picture more lanes opening on a highway.\n- **Voltage is across, current is through.** You measure voltage *between two points*, current *through* a wire.")
 ],
 "practice": [
  NUM("A 12 V battery drives current through a 4 Ω resistor. Current?", 3, "I = V ÷ R = 12 ÷ 4 = 3 A.", unit="A"),
  NUM("A device draws 2 A on a 120 V circuit. Its power?", 240, "P = V·I = 120 × 2 = 240 W.", unit="W"),
  NUM("Resistors of 5 Ω, 10 Ω and 15 Ω are in **series**. Total resistance?", 30, "Series: add them. 5 + 10 + 15 = 30 Ω.", unit="Ω"),
  NUM("Two 10 Ω resistors in **parallel**. Total resistance?", 5, "Two equal resistors in parallel: half of one. 1/R = 1/10 + 1/10 = 2/10 → R = 5 Ω.", unit="Ω"),
  MC("Houses are wired in parallel so that…", ["every device gets the full voltage and works independently", "current is the same everywhere", "power bills are lower", "fewer wires are needed"], 0, "Each branch gets 120 V, and one device off doesn't shut the rest down."),
  MC("What does a circuit breaker protect?", ["People from shocks", "The wiring from overheating", "Devices from low voltage", "The power company's meter"], 1, "Breakers trip on too much current to protect **wires**. GFCIs protect **people**."),
  NUM("A 100 W bulb runs for 10 hours. Energy used, in kWh?", 1, "0.1 kW × 10 h = 1 kWh.", unit="kWh"),
  MC("Two 12 V batteries connected in **series** give…", ["12 V", "24 V", "6 V", "144 V"], 1, "In series, voltages add."),
  NUM("A 60 W bulb runs on 120 V. What is its resistance?", 240,
      "First I = P ÷ V = 60 ÷ 120 = 0.5 A. Then R = V ÷ I = 120 ÷ 0.5 = **240 Ω**. (Or in one step: R = V² ÷ P = 14,400 ÷ 60.)",
      level="challenge", hint="Find the current first with P = V·I.", unit="Ω",
      steps=["I = P ÷ V = 60 ÷ 120 = 0.5 A.", "R = V ÷ I = 120 ÷ 0.5 = 240 Ω."]),
  NUM("A 20 A breaker on a 120 V circuit. What's the most total power you can run before it trips?", 2400,
      "P = V·I = 120 × 20 = **2,400 W**. A 1,500 W heater plus a 1,200 W microwave (2,700 W) will trip it.",
      level="challenge", hint="P = V·I with the breaker's current limit.", unit="W"),
  MC("You plug a second space heater into the same parallel circuit. What happens to the **total current** drawn from the outlet?", ["It drops", "It stays the same", "It increases", "It becomes zero"], 2,
     "Each branch draws its own current, and branch currents **add**. Another path also means lower total resistance, so I = V ÷ R goes up.",
     level="challenge", hint="In parallel, do branch currents add or split?"),
  MC("Why do power lines carry electricity at very high voltage?", ["High voltage travels faster", "For the same power, high voltage means low current, and heat lost in the wires grows with current squared", "It's safer for birds", "High voltage needs no wires"], 1,
     "P = V·I, so raising V lowers I for the same power. Losses are I²·R: cut the current by 10 and losses drop by 100.",
     level="challenge", hint="Look at P = I²·R. What happens to losses if current goes down?"),
 ],
 "sources": [OSP, OSCP, {"title": "OSHA: Electrical safety", "url": "https://www.osha.gov/electrical"}]})

